      "RQ2": {
        "answer": "*Available Barclays coverage is from June 12 to September 29, 2025.*\n\n| Report date | Rating | Price-target revision | Increase | Implied upside at publication |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Jun. 17, 2025 | Overweight, unchanged | **$170 → $200** | **18%** | 38.2% [Barclays, 2025-06-17, p.1] |\n| Sep. 25, 2025 | Overweight, unchanged | **$200 → $240** | **20%** | 35.6% [Barclays, 2025-09-25, p.1] |\n\n**Yes—Barclays has been getting more bullish.** It raised Nvidia’s target twice in roughly three months, from $170 to $240—a cumulative **$70, or 41%** increase—while retaining its Overweight rating.\n\nThe rationale also strengthened:\n\n- **June:** better second-half supply-chain builds, higher compute estimates and confidence in Blackwell/Blackwell Ultra drove the move to $200 [Barclays, 2025-06-17, p.1].\n- **September:** Barclays estimated more than **$2T of planned AI infrastructure spending**, with roughly 65–70% attributable to compute and networking, and expected much of that opportunity to flow through Nvidia over five-plus years [Barclays, 2025-09-25, p.1].\n\nOne nuance: implied upside slipped from 38.2% to 35.6% despite the higher target because Nvidia’s share price had risen substantially between the reports. Thus, the **fundamental view became more bullish**, even though point-in-time forecast upside remained broadly mid-to-high 30%.",
